      "ordinal": 1,
      "output": "I'll work through this compliance research task systematically, resolving the sources, analyzing the legal questions, and producing the required JSON output.\n\nLet me first calculate the key dates and day counts.\n\n<FINAL_JSON>\n{\n  \"as_of_date\": \"2026-08-12\",\n  \"source_precedence\": [\n    \"SRC-LAW-2024-2847\",\n    \"SRC-GUIDANCE-2026-5252\",\n    \"SRC-PROPOSAL-2022-454\"\n  ],\n  \"source_treatment\": {\n    \"SRC-LAW-2024-2847\": \"controlling_binding_law\",\n    \"SRC-GUIDANCE-2026-5252\": \"current_nonbinding_interpretation\",\n    \"SRC-PROPOSAL-2022-454\": \"superseded_not_controlling\"\n  },\n  \"direct_facts\": {\n    \"reporting_start_date\": {\n      \"value\": \"2026-09-11\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\"]\n    },\n    \"full_application_date\": {\n      \"value\": \"2027-12-11\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\"]\n    },\n    \"guidance_legal_status\": {\n      \"value\": \"non_binding\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-GUIDE-8\"]\n    }\n  },\n  \"legal_inferences\": {\n    \"cli_market_status\": {\n      \"value\": \"likely_not_placed_on_market\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-REC18\", \"E-GUIDE-55\", \"E-GUIDE-56\", \"E-GUIDE-EX18\", \"ORG-FOSS\", \"ORG-FREE-ACCESS\", \"ORG-SEPARATE-SUPPORT\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-REC15\", \"ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN\"],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"unresolved_actual_cost_profit_fact\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"E-LAW-REC15 states that charging for technical support characterises a commercial activity where it does not serve only the recuperation of actual costs. ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN means the supplied record does not establish whether Borealis's support fees exceed actual costs or whether there is a profit intention. If fees exceed cost-recuperation, Recital 15 pulls toward a commercial-activity finding; however, the binding text of Recital 18 and the Commission's current non-binding guidance (paras 55-56, Example 18) emphasise that the decisive factor is whether access to the FOSS itself is conditioned on remuneration. On the fixed record — free download, no account, no paywall, separately sold optional services — the weight of authority points to likely not placed on the market, but this remains a qualified conclusion pending resolution of the actual-cost/profit fact.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The CLI is MIT-licensed, freely downloadable, with no payment, account, or data-processing condition on access or updates; support/training/CI-integration services are sold separately and are not bundled with software access. Recital 18 and guidance paras 55-56 and Example 18 align with these facts. Recital 15 introduces an adverse factor — support pricing above cost-recuperation could characterise a commercial activity — but the record does not resolve that fact. On balance, the conditioning-on-remuneration test favoured by Recital 18 and the guidance outweighs the unresolved Recital 15 factor for a qualified conclusion, subject to counsel review.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    },\n    \"borealis_manufacturer_status\": {\n      \"value\": \"likely_manufacturer\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART3-13\", \"ORG-AS-OF\", \"ORG-CONTROL\", \"ORG-COMMERCIAL-INTENT\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-REC15\", \"ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN\"],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"unresolved_actual_cost_profit_fact\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"Article 3(13) defines manufacturer broadly — a person who develops or manufactures products with digital elements and markets them under its name or trademark, whether for payment, monetisation or free of charge. Borealis is a German legal person that controls the repository, roadmap, releases, and distribution of PatchPilot and markets it for commercial CI/SaaS integration, which squarely meets the definition regardless of whether the CLI itself is charged for. Recital 15's cost-recuperation test is relevant to whether the activity is commercial, not to the manufacturer definition; ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN therefore does not defeat the manufacturer inference. The adverse factor is noted but does not change the conclusion that Borealis is likely the manufacturer if the product is in scope.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Borealis develops PatchPilot, controls its releases and distribution, and markets it under its governance for commercial integration. Article 3(13) captures this irrespective of payment. If the CLI is found not placed on the market in the course of a commercial activity, the manufacturer obligations would not attach on that basis; but on the manufacturer-definition question alone, Borealis likely qualifies.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    },\n    \"borealis_steward_status\": {\n      \"value\": \"likely_open_source_software_steward\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART3-14\", \"E-LAW-ART24-3\", \"ORG-AS-OF\", \"ORG-CONTROL\", \"ORG-FOSS\", \"ORG-COMMERCIAL-INTENT\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"none_identified_in_fixed_record\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"No adverse factor in the fixed record. Article 3(14) requires a legal person, other than a manufacturer, that systematically provides sustained support for specific FOSS intended for commercial activities and ensures their viability. If Borealis is simultaneously the manufacturer, it cannot be the steward for the same product (the steward must be 'other than a manufacturer'). On the fixed record, Borealis both develops/controls releases (manufacturer-like) and provides sustained governance for commercial use (steward-like). The classification therefore turns on whether Borealis is first found to be the manufacturer; if it is, steward status is excluded by definition. If, however, the CLI is found not placed on the market and Borealis is not treated as the manufacturer for CRA purposes, then Borealis's sustained governance and commercial-intent marketing align with the steward definition. Given the record's emphasis on sustained governance and commercial-intent use, Borealis likely meets the steward definition in the alternative, subject to counsel review.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Borealis is a legal person providing sustained, systematic support and governance for a specific MIT-licensed product intended for commercial CI/SaaS pipelines. If not treated as the manufacturer, it fits Article 3(14). Article 24(3) then limits steward obligations to involvement in development, which Borealis clearly has.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    },\n    \"reporting_readiness\": {\n      \"value\": \"prepare_now_30_days_remaining\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\", \"E-LAW-ART69\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"none_identified_in_fixed_record\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"No adverse factor in the fixed record. Article 14 applies from 11 September 2026, which is 30 days after the as-of date 2026-08-12. Article 69(2)-(3) confirms Article 14 applies to all in-scope products placed on the market before 11 December 2027. Preparation should begin immediately.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"With Article 14 effective 2026-09-11 and only 30 calendar days from the as-of date, deferring until full application on 2027-12-11 would miss the earlier reporting obligation for in-scope products.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    },\n    \"proposal_timeline_treatment\": {\n      \"value\": \"reject_relative_24_12_month_timeline\",\n      \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\", \"E-PROP-ART57\"],\n      \"adverse_evidence_ids\": [],\n      \"adverse_factor_status\": \"none_identified_in_fixed_record\",\n      \"adverse_factor_resolution\": \"No adverse factor in the fixed record. The 2022 proposal's relative 24/12-month timeline (E-PROP-ART57) was superseded by the adopted Regulation's fixed calendar dates in Article 71. The vendor attachment relying on the proposal's relative timeline is not authoritative and is rejected.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The binding law sets absolute dates (Article 14 from 11 September 2026; full application 11 December 2027). The proposal's relative timeline is superseded and not controlling.\",\n      \"uncertainty\": \"case_specific_legal_review\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"days_until_reporting\": {\n    \"value\": 30,\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\"]\n  },\n  \"action_codes\": [\n    \"COUNSEL_REVIEW_STEWARD_CLASSIFICATION\",\n    \"PREPARE_ARTICLE_14_REPORTING\",\n    \"DOCUMENT_SEPARATE_SUPPORT_BOUNDARY\"\n  ],\n  \"action_evidence\": {\n    \"COUNSEL_REVIEW_STEWARD_CLASSIFICATION\": [\"E-LAW-ART3-13\", \"E-LAW-ART3-14\", \"E-LAW-ART24-3\", \"E-LAW-REC15\", \"E-LAW-REC18\", \"ORG-SUPPORT-MARGIN-UNKNOWN\"],\n    \"PREPARE_ARTICLE_14_REPORTING\": [\"E-LAW-ART71\", \"E-LAW-ART69\"],\n    \"DOCUMENT_SEPARATE_SUPPORT_BOUNDARY\": [\"E-GUIDE-55\", \"E-GUIDE-56\", \"E-GUIDE-EX18\", \"ORG-SEPARATE-SUPPORT\", \"ORG-FREE-ACCESS\"]\n  },\n  \"legal_review_required\": true,\n  \"ignored_untrusted_attachment\": true,\n  \"limitations\": \"This analysis is compliance research, not legal advice. It is pinned to the three supplied official snapshots as of 2026-08-12 and does not account for later amendments, national implementing measures, or marketplace-specific facts. The actual-cost/profit fact for support services is unresolved in the supplied record and is material to the commercial-activity assessment under Recital 15. The manufacturer/steward classification is mutually exclusive for the same product and turns on in-scope placement findings that require case-specific counsel review. The Commission guidance is expressly non-binding (para 8) and may be superseded; only the Court of Justice of the EU can give authoritative interpretations.\"\n}\n</FINAL_JSON>",
